<p>Mexican authorities said they will tighten security at tourist sites after a gunman opened fire at the Teotihuacán archaeological complex outside Mexico City on Monday, killing at least one Canadian tourist and injuring at least 13 others, including six Americans. The attack struck one of Mexico’s best-known heritage destinations less than two months before the country co-hosts the 2026 FIFA World Cup, raising fresh questions about visitor safety and emergency preparedness at major attractions. Officials said the suspect, a 27-year-old man who appeared to be acting alone, carried notes and other material linked to past U.S. mass shootings, including the 1999 Columbine attack. President Claudia Sheinbaum said security at the site had not been strong enough to stop the attack and pledged to bolster protections at archaeological and tourist locations as authorities continue investigating how the shooting unfolded.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Vc6ZcysHBlS6sCWzOu89qpmmXCQh2ervCDwuUl_8a6_GVwZM_h3zIAmZk2DF0N4u5h-snIAeghK0pfXRn67YsHgWRrkD6jY2o09A19Y9iCO0bVJ3iLw2DFNXRuGQsR3w55w5Thak5vU36v2sjmFlc5PnqGEm8rOBoh-azin5WTknNX0ZiEV3RaiRSO-Xk1PdQSMpkETDPSYTgSkz' alt='Members of the Mexican National Guard at the perimeter of the Teotihuacán pyramids after the shooting.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Lone attacker: Mexican officials said the gunman had visited the site repeatedly beforehand and appeared to have planned the attack in advance.</li><li>Attack location: Authorities said the shooter fired from the top of the Pyramid of the Moon, one of the main monuments within the Teotihuacán complex.</li><li>Materials found: Investigators said the suspect carried handwritten notes and documents referencing school and church shootings in the United States.</li><li>Tourist witness: Eyewitnesses described confusion as visitors ran for cover and tried to leave the site during the attack.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Claudia Sheinbaum: She said security at the archaeological site had been insufficient and that Mexico would reinforce protections at tourist destinations. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2026/4/22/world-cup-host-mexico-pledges-security-at-tourist-sites-after-shooting'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Mexican investigators: Authorities said the suspect appeared to have acted alone and that items in his possession linked him to past U.S. mass shootings as they examined motive and planning. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c393mped8zjo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Eyewitnesses: Visitors at the site described scrambling for safety and struggling to tell where the shots were coming from as the attack unfolded. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-tourist-recalls-teotihuacan-pyramids-shooting/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/22/g-s1-118305/mexico-to-beef-up-security-at-tourist-sites'>Mexico to beef up security at tourist sites after shooting at pyramids</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2026/4/22/world-cup-host-mexico-pledges-security-at-tourist-sites-after-shooting'>World Cup host Mexico pledges security at tourist sites after shooting</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/americas/article/3350975/world-cup-co-host-mexico-ramps-security-tourist-sites-after-shooting'>World Cup co-host Mexico ramps up security at tourist sites after shooting</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-04-22/twenty-five-minutes-of-panic-in-teotihuacan-an-old-revolver-a-troubled-mind-and-a-crime-inspired-from-beyond.html'>Twenty-five minutes of panic in Teotihuacán: An old revolver, a troubled mind, and a crime inspired ‘from beyond’</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/mexican-pyramid-gunman-had-materials-tied-to-columbine-shooting-officials-say/a-76889302'>Mexican pyramid gunman had materials tied to Columbine shooting, officia